{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1458165-necromancy-or-thaumaturgy","id":1458165,"title":"Necromancy or Thaumaturgy","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-08-31T12:37:21-07:00","id":1458165,"title":"Necromancy or Thaumaturgy","content":"If you could learn one of the two (you have a teacher for each of them but choosing one prevents you from learning the other), which one would you choose and for what reason?<br /> I honestly find myself incapable of making a choice as both have some appeal. For necromancy, it might be the fact that I have never played a necromancer in Vampire and it presents the extra appeal of the unknown and opening up so many new possibilities. <br /> <br /> So which one would you choose?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26925-denstoffy","name":"Denstoffy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26925_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-31T12:50:27-07:00","id":1458167,"title":"","content":"As much as I dig the concepts of zombies and ghosts, I couldn\u2019t see giving up Movement if the Mind, Path of Mercury and/or Path of Conjuring to play with zombies and ghosts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24351-garygeneric","name":"Garygeneric","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24351_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"108"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-31T12:54:59-07:00","id":1458168,"title":"","content":"Necromancy.<br /> Sure, Thaumaturgy is versatile, but so are a wraith's arcanoi. And I have so may terrible, stupid ideas for pranks involving zombies. Besides, free access to the Underworld! A terrible, terrible place for a trip so obviously an ideal place to spend the holidays with the family. I also have a special place in my heart for the Corpse in the Monster path, for some reason. It's all about the style, and necromancy's creepy macabre edgelord-ness and the research into death and the undead state is more my alley than the Hermetic paradigm. Plus Wraith is my second favourite line, so it lets me blend.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":26836,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","user":"Rhywbeth","date":". 08-31-2021, 04:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-31T13:06:03-07:00","id":1458170,"title":"","content":"Necromancy all the freaking way. Not only can you either make useful servants, Necromancy also allows you to enter the Low Umbra and generally the Underworld as well. You can make a base in an area where only a very small amount of other Kindred even go into. Not only that, putting Wraiths in your service basically can net you potential soldiers that cannot be seen and cannot be striked back against.<br /> <br /> A Wraith with Embody under your servitude can also literally make one of the best assassin's in World of Darkness because there is little counter an opponent can do, when they are stealth attacked from something coming out of the Umbra behind you with chance of knowing whats going on. It's basically akin to how the Garou can sidestep into the Middle Umbra and sidestep back out for an easy stealth kill.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Jade Kingdom Warrior","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25523-shakanaka","name":"Shakanaka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"951"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-31T13:19:28-07:00","id":1458171,"title":"","content":"Oddly imagining if this was applied to me as opposed to a character I'd make: <br /> <br /> Advantages of Necromancy<br /> -Doesn't use much blood. This is a godly advantage for mortal learners, since blood means health levels for them.<br /> -More front-loading. Talking to ghosts is something you can learn early on, and it's a really good skill. Most of the best thaumaturgy stuff is higher level.<br /> <br /> Advantages of Thaumaturgy<br /> -Self improvement.<br /> -far more possibilities<br /> -Not fucking corpses.<br /> <br /> Things to consider:<br /> What's my willpower score?<br /> Am I a vampire for this question? Yes brings me to thaumaturgy, no brings me to necromancy.<br /> What would make me more money? I wouldn't mind becoming a public figure, but easy cash is good too.<br /> <br /> I lean more to thaumaturgy. It's more positive, empowering, and I wouldn't have to spend lots of time around death. But if I didn't have much talent/capacity for it, i'd rather make money from ghosts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25794,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","user":"MyWifeIsScary","date":". 08-31-2021, 04:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-31T15:14:28-07:00","id":1458180,"title":"","content":"Each has its pros and cons as others have stated, but a lot of it comes down to how the ST in the game is handling certain aspects of the setting.<br /> <br /> Thaumaturgy is a tool kit where each path does one specific thing very well, thus given enough time and XP you can become very powerful. Conversely Necromancy is a bit more limited in scope, with much fewer Paths or rituals, which means it functions more like a multi-tool or utility tool which can do many jobs good enough though not great. But this means it is time and XP cost efficient and perhaps most importantly it provides upfront power at a faster rate than Thaumaturgy with its long haul investment requirement.<br /> <br /> The Sepulcher Path at level three, i.e. at character generation, allows you to summon ghosts and compel them. This at first glance means you can talk to the dead and maybe get some minions. But technically you can build a spy network, each ghost can be a teacher or advisor, each ghost can be an assassin, etc. This far outstrips the power curve for almost any other single thaumaturgy path with only three dots in it. The path of Technomancy and the path of Conjuring being the main competitors for versatility at only three dots.<br /> <br /> But if the ST is being problematic, then the only ghosts around have bad stats, were children when they died and thus don't have professional grade skills or knowledge, or even worse the only spirits around are very powerful specters which means you don't have the ability to tangle with them. Conversely most Thaumaturgy paths are very straight forward with specific mechanics, Path of Flames will always light things on fire, Movement of the Mind will always move things that are inside its weight limit. So the ST is unlikely to curtail your use of the Thaumaturgy.<br /> <br /> Now some things at first glance some Paths or Rituals appear to give the edge to one or the other, but usually there are ways to balance it out. Thamaturgy has Wards which make for automatic protection, but the Sepulcher path means you can have dozens of ghostly body guards in the same room as you and your enemies won't know it until it is too late. Thaumaturgy has limited teleportation with Escape to a True Friend, but the Necromancy Ash Path allows you to enter the Underworld itself which means you can bypass most mortal world threats.<br /> <br /> So it really comes down to how much leeway your ST gives your Necromancer or how much time and XP your ST will allow you to spend on Thaumaturgy before you have to depend on it to survive.<br /> <br /> Or to contextualize it another way, Thaumaturgy is the anime &quot;<a href=\"http://I&#39;ve Been Killing Slimes for 300 Years and Maxed Out My Level\" target=\"_blank\">I've Been Killing Slimes for 300 Years and Maxed Out My Level</a>&quot; where XP grinding without serious threat is the way of life. While Necromancy would be something like &quot;<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berserk_(manga)\" target=\"_blank\">Berserk</a>&quot; anime where everything is messy and depressing, but the answer is always a big sword.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-31T15:40:49-07:00","id":1458185,"title":"","content":"What ed? Because Thaum in v5 is a waste of xp.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24832-blailton","name":"blailton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24832_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"389"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-31T16:39:49-07:00","id":1458194,"title":"","content":"Well obviously, probably the older editions, considering Necromancy and its subsequent Paths were removed in V5 for the &quot;Oblivion&quot; Discipline nonsense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Jade Kingdom Warrior","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25523-shakanaka","name":"Shakanaka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"951"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-31T20:14:37-07:00","id":1458207,"title":"","content":"Necromancy for sure. While there are some incredibly cool Thaumaturgy Paths, being able to speak with and interact with ghosts, some of whom might be centuries old, would amazing. Plus summoning up ghosts with certain Arcanoi can sort of allow you to pull off similar shenanigans as some Thaumaturgy Paths. Like you might not have the Path of Technomancy, but you could have a ghost with Inhabit possess a computer or a smart phone to similar effect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-01T01:38:30-07:00","id":1458215,"title":"","content":"As a person rather than a VtM character? Thaumaturgy. I <i>like</i> Necromancy. I like it a lot. But I am not a slaver. And enslaving sentient beings is kind of the foundational premise of the coolest branch - Sepulcher.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12892-kammerer","name":"Kammerer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12892_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-01T06:59:13-07:00","id":1458224,"title":"","content":"Enslaving ghosts is something Necromancy can do but it's not like that's the only way of engaging with them. The Samedi for example have a lot of mutual respect for ghosts in the Underworld and tend to treat them more as partners than as disposable tools. It's just that the asshole Giovanni were mostly the face of Necromancy for so long and their style was particularly fucked up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-01T16:12:49-07:00","id":1458266,"title":"","content":"The thinking girls answer-Mortis.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-01T19:25:52-07:00","id":1458291,"title":"","content":"I think Thaumaturgy has more application in the game.<br /> <br /> For me, the only reason you want a character to know Necromancy is if you want that character (or the game) to explore the world of Wraith. You find ghosts fascinating and want to explore that in the setting somehow. If so, that's great and it's a valid choice if your ST is ready. If you are not interested in exploring the underworld, its hazards, perils, mysteries, etc. don't go to Necromancy.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately in my experience, most Vampire STs have a limited understanding of Wraith and don't want to take the time to master the Wraith setting. I don't think that's a fault. The Vampire setting on its own is a huge thing, and you shouldn't feel compelled to go out and buy even more books just to scratch someone else's itch. Unfortunately they allow the PC to buy Necromancy anyway and then half-ass it. That allows certain players to abuse it. Usually what I see is that the player wants Necromancy because they think it can be a superweapon that other vampires have no defense against. So I pretty much disagree with most of what Shakanaka posted. Anytime you think there is something that other characters can't counter, the ST needs to stop that. It is bad STing to allow that.<br /> <br /> In fact, there are tons of things that should make Necromancy extremely dangerous for its practitioner. All Disciplines should be useful. None should be impossible to counter. If a player uses it sparingly in a way that doesn't disrupt the game, there's no issue letting him use it. If he wants to create an unstoppable secret assassin that can't be stopped, have an unreachable secret hideout, or lots of enslaved servants that can never be foiled, it's time for the ST to step in and begin to actually roleplay the actual setting of Wraith.<br /> <br /> Wraiths have Shadows and can go into catharsis, and you can bet any wraith enslaved by being tortured and forced into servitude is going to succumb to their Shadow at some point. Their Circle of wraiths will want to help them at some point. Legionary patrols of the Hierarchy or Renegade gangs will encounter you. Your existing enslaved wraiths might get enslaved by another wraith or soulforged. One of the many maelstroms in the Shadowlands hits your hideout and wipes it out, and if you happen to be there your health levels get sheared off and you die. Spectres erupt from a Nihil as you walk past one day. Lots of bad things can happen. Like they would if you played an ordinary Wraith PC in a Wraith chronicle.<br /> <br /> If you're a player who wants to actually explore that, it's all great fun for them as they learn about them and interact with these new NPCs. If all you want out of Necromancy is unstoppable exploits, the player gets very upset because that is not what he wanted.<br /> <br /> Not to mention, even ordinary humans have a lot of defenses against wraiths. You don't need to be supernatural to defend yourself. Most areas will have a high enough Shroud that it becomes very hard to do anything in the Skinlands. In order to Forbid or Ward against wraiths, you just need to think whatever &quot;ritual&quot; you are doing will work for it to work. Anyone with Occult 3 can stop a Wraith dead in their tracks in terms of the Skinlands (which is all that's important). Hell, innocent children can simply tell a wraith to &quot;Go away!&quot; and that works. Animals can detect the presence of wraith because they aren't affected by the Fog. That's the trope of your dog barking at no one. In the game, your dog has sensed a wraith has entered the home and is telling it to go away. It may not do anything to the Wraith, but it could raise the Shroud as the owner focuses their attention to that and don't have time to think its becoming spooky. And in vampire terms, if you have Animalism it can be a very good warning system that allows you to break out that &quot;amulet&quot; you bought from the fortune teller down the street that wards you. You don't need Tremere rituals or anything else. While supernatural creatures are slightly more vulnerable because the Shroud is lowered a bit by their mere presence, they do have more powers available to them. A Necromancer will need to be subtle if he is to make good use of his powers because too obvious uses produces its own backlash.<br /> <br /> Let the PC have their fun and keep Necromancy useful. But no unstoppable super assassins and the like. The setting, if you run it properly, defeats that.<br /> <br /> I know other STs have different tastes and preferences, and that's fine. But that's how I run my games and I think it's very faithful to both the published setting and the genre.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-02T07:23:44-07:00","id":1458344,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1458165-necromancy-or-thaumaturgy?p=1458224#post1458224\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Enslaving ghosts is something Necromancy can do but it's not like that's the only way of engaging with them. The Samedi for example have a lot of mutual respect for ghosts in the Underworld and tend to treat them more as partners than as disposable tools. It's just that the asshole Giovanni were mostly the face of Necromancy for so long and their style was particularly fucked up.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Samedi also don't have Necromancy, so who's going to listen to them?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1458165-necromancy-or-thaumaturgy?p=1458266#post1458266\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The thinking girls answer-Mortis.</div> </div> </div> </div>Meh. Good for a vampire who fights all the time, but what am I supposed to do with Mortis as a still-living office drone? Horrifically curse colleagues for laughs?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12892-kammerer","name":"Kammerer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12892_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-02T07:48:32-07:00","id":1458350,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">So I pretty much disagree with most of what Shakanaka posted. Anytime you think there is something that other characters can't counter, the ST needs to stop that. It is bad STing to allow that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why? If a player works toward an ability that gives them a heightened advantage, then let them take in the spoils of their hard work (probably paid for in chronicle XP) and the benefits of their strategy of how they use that ability. World of Darkness is inherently a deadly world. And its not as if Necromancy cannot be countered, but the person who is being targeted probably would need to know some Necromancy or be in contact with counter-neutral Necromancers to stave off their enemy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wraiths have Shadows and can go into catharsis, and you can bet any wraith enslaved by being tortured and forced into servitude is going to succumb to their Shadow at some point.</div> </div> </div> </div>Touching on the Shadow aspect of a Wraith is a perfectly valid and central point, nothing wrong with this. But you inherently assume the Wraith would be enslaved or could be forced into being an ally for the Necromancer Vampire (I think this partially my fault to begin with, since my post continually mentioned &quot;service&quot; in it. But a service doesn't have to mean its forced. The relationship could be a service-for-a-service type of deal, where the Necromancer does something for the Wraith that feeds into the latter helping the former to begin with).<br /> <br /> Not every Vampire-Wraith relation is like this at all and could be one of a mutual benefit or understanding. Even then, a Catharsis episode lasts for a variable amount of time and it isn't permanent. It isn't the same as being Shadow-Eaten where a Wraith turns in a Spectre. Even then still, its not as if Spectres cannot be reasoned with either, its just that they're more untrustworthy than before, but even as a Spectre, an interest of a mutual partnership may still be in the interest of the Spectre one way or the other.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Their Circle of wraiths will want to help them at some point.</div> </div> </div> </div>Circles aren't that common in the Wraith setting and only seems as such, because the Wraith books go with the default assumption that the ongoing center piece of the chronicle centers around one, due to the fact that the multiple players in an average session are all probably Circlemates. This isn't common to Wraith either, but other lines as well. Only in Werewolf is it fully expected and confirmed both IC and OOC that you'll be in a &quot;team&quot; (Pack) of sorts.<br /> <br /> A Necromancer could just as easily only be contact and deal with one Wraith only; even if said Wraith does have a Circle, as long as the partnership the Necromancer and Wraith have is mutually beneficial and non-hostile or imbalanced, its unlikely the Circle would intervene to mess up their Circlemates affairs (though such a thing CAN easily happen if one of their Circlemates has a Cathartic episode).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Legionary patrols of the Hierarchy or Renegade gangs will encounter you.</div> </div> </div> </div>Legion patrols can and could be a threat of a sorts, but the Wraith being enlisted or hired by the Necromancer would probably be an experienced professional. Especially in a large urban area too, it would be unlikely for them to be caught. Renegades would probably not care at all about a Wraith doing such jobs either unless it directly opposes their interests, and it'd be likely anyway the Wraith the Necromancer is dealing with is probably a Renegade themselves to begin with for doing such jobs.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Your existing enslaved wraiths might get enslaved by another wraith or soulforged.</div> </div> </div> </div>Valid point, no argument there.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">One of the many maelstroms in the Shadowlands hits your hideout and wipes it out, and if you happen to be there your health levels get sheared off and you die.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maelstroms aren't that fast and most Necropolis' having a warning system for an approaching Maelstrom. And Haunts and most buildings in general, are protected easily from Maelstroms. Only Great Maelstroms have the power to destroy things if I remember correctly, but again, it only destroys Shadowland buildings and structures. Regular buildings in the Skinlands aren't affected at all and a Necromancer can just leave the Shadowlands at anytime.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not to mention, even ordinary humans have a lot of defenses against wraiths. You don't need to be supernatural to defend yourself.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not really... no. Most humans don't have many defenses against Wraiths at all. The only stopgap is essentially Wraiths with little to no experience in using their Arcanoi, but those are essentially new arrivals/newly minted ghosts. Any Wraith with any good amount of dots in the specific Arcanoi for influencing the Skinlands (aka haunting something, basically) can do so without having any defense at all.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Most areas will have a high enough Shroud that it becomes very hard to do anything in the Skinlands</div> </div> </div> </div>Well the Wraith can just follow the person their haunting until they wander into an area with a low Shroud rating, then do what they want to do. And even then, per the Shroud chart, the DC to use Arcanoi in say a place like a &quot;A well-cared for home with no violent history&quot; is only 8. A Wraith a with a decent dice pool to work with, can and will in most cases, be able to do something.<br /> <br /> Most Vampires anyway, reside in urban areas/cities. Cities no matter what, are almost always dark in someway and hiding the resonances of a past violence or malignance of somesort. Doubly so in cities with Vampires in them. Shroud ratings in such places are a definite to be low. The Necromancer can just as easily as well, lower the Shroud level in an area to help the Wraith do their task easier.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">In order to Forbid or Ward against wraiths, you just need to think whatever &quot;ritual&quot; you are doing will work for it to work. Anyone with Occult 3 can stop a Wraith dead in their tracks in terms of the Skinlands (which is all that's important).</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah.. but most people wouldn't really have Occult 3 realistically. Niche hobbyists would probably only have 1-2 dots at best. And a Wraith who's serious about killing someone can do so before that person can even get into contact with someone with Occult 3 anyway.<br /> <br /> Forbiddance isn't that strong and purely relies on a Willpower roll at DC 7; and the 2nd Ed book says that a Wraith can easily resist a Forbiddance ritual with an &quot;appropriate Arcanos&quot; in an opposed roll. Unless that person is really strong willed, their Willpower pool wouldn't be enough to stave off a Wraith with decently built-up dots in the Arcanoi their using + the subsequent Attribute traditionally used with that following Arcanoi.<br /> <br /> Ward rituals are much more stronger than Forbiddance, but relies on a Willpower roll at DC 7 and a spent Willpower. The Ward also only lasts for the successes acquired in the roll, however, which wouldn't be much if the Warder's Willpower isn't that much. They can spend a permanent Willpower to extend the Ward for months up to a year, but that's very costly and taxing, and makes them much weaker long-term.<br /> <br /> But overall, Ward isn't that much of a stopgap because the DC to pass through it, is the permanent Willpower score of the mortal who set it. It'd especially be more weaker to begin with, if the mortal who set it up spent a permanent Willpower to makes its duration last longer too. Though to pass it, the attempting Wraith would need to roll their own Willpower to negate the Ward; and even if they pass, Arcanoi in the breached ward area are at +2 DC.. so Ward is halfway decent, but not good in most areas in deterrence.<br /> <br /> The Wraith could just wait it out. The target will eventually need to either go out; food, water, the need to go to work or get fired, etc. Wards also need to be renewed, which would be very taxing on the targeted victim and old wards need to be taken down for a new ward to be implemented. In the timeline between the decommissioning of the old ward and setting up a new one, a Wraith can easily disrupt the ritual after knowing the tactic of the target at this point. Lastly, the focuses for the ward ritual itself (a circle, incense, or candles) can be directly attacked or targeted. Those go out and the ward fails.<br /> <br /> And again, this all predicates that most people have Occult 3 or above or even believed in ghosts before being haunted.<br /> <br /> So yeah, most humans don't have any reliable defenses against Wraiths; except those with &quot;Innocence&quot;, who can insta-compel a Wraith to leave them alone. But &quot;Innocence&quot; as Wraith describes it only is in children or very sheltered people or in some facets of the insane.<br /> <br /> The rest of your last points are valid and sensical tactics to counter Wraiths in some fashion, so I largely agree with them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Jade Kingdom Warrior","edited":{"userid":25523,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25523-shakanaka","user":"Shakanaka","date":". 09-02-2021, 10:57 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25523-shakanaka","name":"Shakanaka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"951"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-02T08:57:28-07:00","id":1458359,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shakanaka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1458165-necromancy-or-thaumaturgy?p=1458350#post1458350\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why? If a player works toward an ability that gives them a heightened advantage, then let them take in the spoils of their hard work (probably paid for in chronicle XP) and the benefits of their strategy of how they use that ability.</div> </div> </div> </div>One thing is for the player to work toward a sensible strategy and set of abilities, another is for <i>Necromancy 3</i> be the whole of that strategy. A player that have Necromancy + abilities of interest to Wraiths + fair knowledge of local Wraiths + works a lot towards establishing those pacts is a very different animal from &quot;Necromancy 3 does that&quot;.<br /> <br /> But directly answering to your question, why: because it makes the game dull and hard for the ST to conduct good stories. Note that Black Fox specifically talked about making Necromancy impossible to counter, in this regard. If a single ability is impossible to counter, then that's pretty much going to screw most games. Not a strategy, and not hard to counter, but a single Discipline being impossible to counter in any other way but having the same Discipline.<br /> <br /> And then comes his other arguments, that can be summed up as follows:<br /> <br /> If both Player and ST know Wraith and the consequences and limitations of Necromancy, and want to play the part, then its fine.<br /> <br /> If any or both of Player and ST don't know Wraith, but the two come to an agreement about what ghosts can and cannot do in the chronicle, and there are ways to counter them without necessary access to Necromancy, then it is also fine.<br /> <br /> If ghosts can do whatever shit the player wants and there is no catch or counter, then that' not fine.<br /> <br /> If Necromancy were the only counter possible against ghosts, the entire setting would be a very distinct place as it would be the single most important Discipline for any faction to have access to.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-02T10:01:07-07:00","id":1458374,"title":"","content":"For what it is worth another aspect that Necromancy tends to bring up is the play style Players and STs want to use for in game conflicts.<br /> <br /> Some people want the power fantasy of being the combat machine, thus their build starts and ends with Fortitude, Celerity, Potence, and maybe Protean for agg damage claws. However other people prefer the power fantasy of never having to get their hands dirty because they win before combat ever happens. This can be achieved by several different methods, social builds who pull in allies, background builds who manipulate the setting rather than the characters, or by being a summoner who uses minions. The problem is that when a player or ST wants to go one way with the game and it negates some one else's preference, the salt will overflow very quickly.<br /> <br /> Necromancy brings this to the forefront very quickly. Sure your brawler might be a badass, but when a ghost can set your haven on fire in the middle of the day you have no idea what happened, that an attack was coming, who is responsible, or what to do about it. This can be seen as boring to some or game breaking to others.<br /> <br /> But in truth, you could just as easily use Path of Conjuring to create a drone laden with explosives/incendiaries to do the same thing with a delay timer. Like wise you could use animalism to have various vermin literally undermine or destroy a building over time. Conversely a character with Influence, Contacts, and Resources could just foreclose on the haven and have it demolished. What is the vampire going to do, call the cops or try to deal with it in day time court?<br /> <br /> There are plenty of builds and playstyles that &quot;win&quot; without having to actively fight, but often people tend to forget about that style of play for players, and only use it for NPCs to punish or grief the players. From a historical perspective these win without fighting styles is a lot like bringing a sniper rifle to a knife fight, sure a knife never runs out of bullets, but it can't do much when you can snipe a target up to two miles away. But if a sniper is every finds themselves within knife fighting range, then they have made a probably lethal mistake.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-02T11:20:48-07:00","id":1458385,"title":"","content":"This have nothing to do with win without fighting. Every one of your examples have a chance of being detected and dealt with without requiring a specific power, need more complex action than using a single &quot;power&quot; on your sheet, and may backfire in some way, requiring the &quot;user&quot; to think through before doing that. None of them is a single characteristic becoming an instant win if the other party do not have the same characteristic.<br /> <br /> By this account, the only play style conducive of using Necromancy as such a powerful Discipline would be one where everyone recognizes Necromancy is powerful and everyone have or badly wants access to it. This is, sincerely, quite limiting and boring.<br /> <br /> And again, the problem is with the notion that Necromancy is almost impossible for non-necromancers to deal with in any way. If your interpretation of Necromancy is that it is a super good Discipline that no one can really defend against without specifically acquiring the same Discipline, then it is broken and makes for a lame game. If it isn't, then there is no problem.<br /> <br /> Overall I like Necromancy myself, it is very interesting as a Discipline.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-02T11:49:52-07:00","id":1458391,"title":"","content":"I feel that Necromancy is very much countered by Auspex (Thaumaturgy hard-counters it at later levels) <br /> The main threat is ghosts, but you can use auspex to find ghosts and find fetters, and thus threaten fetters. So a good chunk of &quot;normal&quot; kindred can counter the most powerful aspect of necromancy to an extent. The real issue for me is, well, wraith rules are a bit much to get into for many ST's and even after a shallow reading it's a bit difficult to gauge how powerful ghosts should be. <br /> <br /> <br /> Also necromancy is... dirty. There isn't a clan with charisma that practices.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-02T13:25:22-07:00","id":1458402,"title":"","content":"Shakanaka and I have different interpretations of how things go in the Wraith setting. He ignores or minimizes all the things in the setting that would make it hard for his points. I take them seriously or even emphasize them. All my points stand. It depends on the ST and what the ST allows. Disciplines should be useful and fun for PCs. But if you are at the point where you think you have something that can't countered or is unstoppable, it's a good sign you are probably ignoring a lot.<br /> <br /> It is very easy for an ordinary person to get in contact with someone who has Occult 3 and can perform a Warding or Forbiddance. What would be one of the first things someone does in real life if they think their house is being haunted and are at the point that they're fearful? Most of the time they are going to contact their local religious leader and ask for help. Even if the leader is unqualified, he'll have enough contacts in his religious grouping to easily find someone who can perform an exorcism or similar ritual. Even if all it is holding a crucifix and saying the prayer of St. Michael (real Vatican trained exorcists likely have Occult 4 or 5 with a specialty and likely have access to Inquisition paths). This is what happens in practically every real world case of a bad haunting. <br /> <br /> There are lots of people, especially in the WoD, who will have Occult 3 - religious figures, paranormal enthusiasts, lay magicians, fortune tellers, academics, ghost hunters, wizened old men and women who remember folklore from the old country, etc. Not to mention character concepts that don't have supernatural powers in the real world, but would have real powers in the WoD - priests and ministers with True Faith, mediums who can contact the dead, minor hedge wizards who actually have Paths of sorcery. Not to mention really heavy hunters around in the Inquisition and other groups who could loan out protective amulets, good luck charms, blessed rosaries, etc.. All of that is easily justifiable and consistent with the setting.<br /> <br /> I have no problem with their being bad hauntings or even the occasional person being killed in one. They make good stories (even if every example of a &quot;real life&quot; bad haunting like Amityville is probably a hoax, I have no problem with them happening on occasion in the WoD setting). But the idea that wraiths are simply able to kill whoever they want in the Skinlands is fraught with problems. When it happens, it's not in the spectacular manner Shakanaka seems to imply. And it's probably less efficient than what a vampire could arrange on his own with Necromancy. Wraiths can be useful to a vampire, but the more he uses them or the more obvious use of them are done, the more problems the Necromancer will create for himself because other aspects of the setting will begin to get involved.<br /> <br /> In so far they may be some incredibly well trained Shroudbreaker haunter assassin somewhere in the Underworld, it'll take a lot of work for a Necromancer to learn who they are and successfully (and secretly) contact them, he won't be a &quot;servant&quot; but a professional selling his services, the price for those services will be extremely high, and the Necromancy will not be able to afford such services very often. The chances for failure and blowback at any point of the process is fairly high. Would it be harder or easier than finding another kind of supernatural assassin among the Assamites, Nephandi, lupines, PENTEX or someone else?<br /> <br /> Would I allow a PC to attempt to do this in one of my chronicles? Of course. Would I allow him to succeed? He probably wouldn't. I'd be fair though and allow him to explore the setting, working with local wraiths to find out about secrets like the Haunters guild, avoid the secret police of the Hierarchy, navigate potential treachery and informers, make contact, negotiate price, etc. all with the possibility that something can go wrong (and likely would but depends on how smart he plays and how lucky the dice rolls are). I don't think many vampire players would be interested in that approach as it is taking them away from the Vampire setting. But if a PC is interested in that and has the patience for it, I'd be fine running it. But the end result will not be an unstoppable assassin who cannot be countered whenever he needs one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-02T15:45:37-07:00","id":1458420,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">A player that have Necromancy + abilities of interest to Wraiths + fair knowledge of local Wraiths + works a lot towards establishing those pacts is a very different animal from &quot;Necromancy 3 does that&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>All of which I already alluded to; that a Necromancer can readily make a deal with an interested Wraith who'd want to do a deal and then can form up to be allies. You wouldn't even need Necromancy 3, which is a slight exaggeration on your part. Necromancy 1 could all that'd be needed to commune with a Wraith, anything above is mostly stuff to compel them against their will, but such things wouldn't be needed if you make a straight deal with them only. <br /> <br /> And to begin with, to touch base on the overall matter anyway, my first post only put as far as &quot;one of the of the best assassin's in World of Darkness because there is little counter an opponent can do.&quot; I never claimed as far that it'd be the impossible to counter, just one that has little chance of one to be countered. And there are more other strategies and a boat-load of other Disciplines that in many situations, have little counters against in moment as well. Also, a Wraith who manifests in the Skinlands can still be attacked. If the target survives the first surprise Ambush turn, combat still goes on usual and the Embodied Wraith could have a real risk of being Destruction Harrowed. Though its kinda iffy if its against a mundane human opponent, but the chance of being beat is higher if the target is another supernatural; which is something Black Fox already broached on already in his posts.<br /> <br /> A Wraith ally who can do a stealth strike from the Shadowland to the Skinlands, isn't really ostensibly different from a loyal Ghoul on a rooftop equipped with a high-calibre sniper to kill one of your enemies. Both can't be easily countered in the moment; and that's under the assumption the target is another Kindred. A human shot with a sniper round to the head has no chance of doing a Soak roll at all. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">He ignores or minimizes all the things in the setting that would make it hard for his points.</div> </div> </div> </div>I actually find the inverse in a funny way. It seems you like to lowball Wraiths in general, despite actual material in Wraith suggesting otherwise. Now, I'm not suggesting Wraiths are some uber splat akin to Mages who can do some broken very HAXX effects or a combat arena splat like Werewolf, but they aren't slouches that can relegated as &quot;irritants&quot;. Though indeed, Wraiths can't kill whoever they want - I agree on that. Most Necropolis' are Hierarchy controlled and the Dictiuum Mortem couldn't be something to ignore for long. The more bodies you make, the more likely you'd be investigated essentially. That could be a tough stopgap for the Necromancer's Wraith ally, so call-ins for a job would have to be kept to a minimum or basically be a &quot;nuclear option&quot; so to speak. <br /> <br /> It also is a factor on the exact size of the locale both the Necromancer and Wraith accomplice would be in. A small city would instantly garner a huge controversy if a mysterious, unexplainable murder were to take place. It'd also have to be a one time thing only, because a second mysterious murder by a Wraith would bring in more questions and further intense speculation. If that small city is a Hierarchy owned Necropolis, an active task force would probably be searching for your accomplice. <br /> <br /> A medium city could have some leeway, but at max only two murders/hits could be the amount that could go on, without any suspicion. And that'd have to be spaced at least a few months apart. <br /> <br /> A large city, where most Vampires reside anyway, would be where a Wraith ally could comfortably take on periodic, spaced-out jobs for a Necromancer accomplice without having to move around. Most large cities aren't 100% Hierarchy owned and are divided between either multiple different unaligned/independent/Renegade territories; or held by Heretic factions. It'd be easy to stay under the radar from the Hierarchy in that length. <br /> <br /> Though all the above goes on the assumption on whether or not that city the Necromancer is in, has any Hierarchy presence at all. A certain city, though rarely, could entirely be Renegade controlled or ruled by no faction at all. <br /> <br /> The points about Forbiddance and Wards I already addressed already. They're not really that powerful or do much to impede a Wraith for long. I didn't think of the True Faith angle though and its an interesting aspect I forgot about. Though they didn't say anything about it in the books, I'd think that True Faith could be very damaging to Wraiths and would be a much more competent method than the Forbiddance or Ward techniques in the books. I actually wished they mentioned True Faith in Wraith (or have they already? I'd need to check the 20th ed for Wraith later) so there would be some baselines for scenarios with religious figures, like mentioned in the previous post, to really take hold of and do some critical stuff to a troublesome Wraith.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Jade Kingdom Warrior","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25523-shakanaka","name":"Shakanaka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"951"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-02T16:50:55-07:00","id":1458426,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shakanaka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1458165-necromancy-or-thaumaturgy?p=1458420#post1458420\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I never claimed as far that it'd be the impossible to counter</div> </div> </div> </div>Good. That was the only thing we were trying to argue against, anyway, it seemed to be your point.<br /> <br /> After all, this isn't about Wraith, it's about Vampire. So the question isn't what a Wraith can do, it's what Necromancy can do and if it should be able to do that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-02T16:53:53-07:00","id":1458427,"title":"","content":"Well if you don't even know what a Wraith can do to begin with, why deal with Necromancy in the first place? Such a Discipline, unless your specializing in Zombies and not Ghosts, WILL deal with Wraith.. so your point is moot out the window anyway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Jade Kingdom Warrior","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25523-shakanaka","name":"Shakanaka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"951"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-02T18:19:32-07:00","id":1458436,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1458165-necromancy-or-thaumaturgy?p=1458426#post1458426\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Good. That was the only thing we were trying to argue against, anyway, it seemed to be your point.<br /> <br /> After all, this isn't about Wraith, it's about Vampire. So the question isn't what a Wraith can do, it's what Necromancy can do and if it should be able to do that.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Isn't it basically both? &quot;If I use Necromancy to summon a wraith, what's that wraith going to be able to do?&quot; It's just like if you use Thaumaturgy to summon and bind a spirit you'll want to know what that spirit can do and while we're not playing Werewolf: The Apocalypse, that's a good place to look towards to see what a spirit could reasonably do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-02T21:48:00-07:00","id":1458454,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1458165-necromancy-or-thaumaturgy?p=1458385#post1458385\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This have nothing to do with win without fighting. Every one of your examples have a chance of being detected and dealt with without requiring a specific power, need more complex action than using a single &quot;power&quot; on your sheet, and may backfire in some way, requiring the &quot;user&quot; to think through before doing that. None of them is a single characteristic becoming an instant win if the other party do not have the same characteristic.</div> </div> </div> </div>You missed to concept entirely. When two different ways of doing things come up against each other, one invariably fails or loses. Now this can be due to a lack of awareness, lack of knowledge, or lack of planning. I never claimed Necromancy was an automatic win button, I claimed that it interacts in different ways than most default builds. Thus people don't think about it, even if whether or not you are familiar with Wraith comes into it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1458165-necromancy-or-thaumaturgy?p=1458385#post1458385\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By this account, the only play style conducive of using Necromancy as such a powerful Discipline would be one where everyone recognizes Necromancy is powerful and everyone have or badly wants access to it. This is, sincerely, quite limiting and boring.</div> </div> </div> </div>Dare I say that you have yet to show an example of it being boring or for that matter how normal combat isn't boring. RPGs are about playing with ideas, usually ones we can't or won't do IRL. But if you have had experience within a give field or behavior, it just isn't as fun or cool to do it in game. If you have IRL combat experience, then rpg combat stuff is a lot like listening to a virgin talk about sex.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1458165-necromancy-or-thaumaturgy?p=1458385#post1458385\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And again, the problem is with the notion that Necromancy is almost impossible for non-necromancers to deal with in any way. If your interpretation of Necromancy is that it is a super good Discipline that no one can really defend against without specifically acquiring the same Discipline, then it is broken and makes for a lame game. If it isn't, then there is no problem.</div> </div> </div> </div>Following that logic, any game involving Thaumaturgy, shape shifting, or Dominate is automatically a broken and lame game. Power imbalance is part of what makes a rewarding game play experience when you over come it. Hedge magic, making deals with other necromancers to counter your ghost problem, or just finding a higher powered patron to protect you are all ways of countering necromancy to one degree or another.<br /> <br /> I know you say you like Necromancy, but the way you are arguing this feels like you want a gray box printed in the Necromancy section that says &quot;if you can't figure out how to counter this narratively, do X&quot;, like there has to be a hard counter everyone should have access to because <i><b>reasons</b></i>.<br /> <br /> Personally I like asymmetrical game design over equalitarian game design because it forces you to think about how to leverage abilities or mechanics. It's kind of like how in Poker how while the card combinations technically should determine the outcome of a given hand, in truth the ability to bluff, the size of you chip stack, and most importantly you ability to read people is equally if not more important than the cards in your hand.<br /> <br /> Another aspect of Poker that seems to be lost in vampire games is the difference between the big confrontation and a death of a thousand cuts. Few poker games will have that cinematic finish where one hand determines the outcome of everything. In fact you destroy some one in poker by slowly bleeding them, hand to hand, until their stack is gone. Whether you use Necromancy, Thaumaturgy, Dominate, or just Resources 5 you can beat down a target from multiple vectors before they even realize they are under attack, at which point they have no idea who is doing it or how it is being accomplished, but most importantly if they have become weak enough, then their normal enemies will take advantage of this and strike. Meaning you will cause their fall, but never really have to fight them directly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-03T04:41:48-07:00","id":1458490,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shakanaka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1458165-necromancy-or-thaumaturgy?p=1458427#post1458427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well if you don't even know what a Wraith can do to begin with, why deal with Necromancy in the first place? Such a Discipline, unless your specializing in Zombies and not Ghosts, WILL deal with Wraith.. so your point is moot out the window anyway.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wrong, and this is something you ignored on every post mine and from Black Fox.<br /> <br /> Ghosts aren't Wraiths. Ghosts are whatever the ST wants them to be.<br /> <br /> By the way, there are write-ups for Ghosts in the core V20 book and in some book at every single edition of Vampire.<br /> <br /> You missed the point because you focused on Wraith all the time. There is nothing wrong with you liking the splat and wanting to use it. You are certainly entitled to like Necromancy <b>because</b> it lets you include Wraith in your game. But no one else have to do this and Necromancy's point definitely isn't to do this. It is an option, no less, no more.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar727_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"727\" data-vbnamecard=\"727\">Thoth</a> I misunderstand your previous post. I have no problem with your conclusions at all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":".  typo 09-03-2021, 07:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-03T05:22:23-07:00","id":1458493,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ghosts aren't Wraiths. Ghosts are whatever the ST wants them to be.</div> </div> </div> </div>Clearly the entire whole discussion was about Wraiths and the applications on how they should be used, if needed in a Vampire chronicle dealing with Necromancy. Even before you got involved in it, it was pretty explicitly touching base on Vampire/Wraith and its mechanics. <br /> <br /> But yeah, sure, you can shift goalposts to your desire and make it seem as if we weren't just discussing Wraiths and its subsequent mechanics entire thread, then just boil it down to &quot;Ghosts&quot; (which.. Wraiths are to begin with.. but again, if want to suddenly move goalposts and turn the logic to a &quot;Lupine = Garou&quot; thing and apply this to the previous discussion.. go ahead.. doesn't mean you're halfway correct in matter, however). <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">But no one else have to do this</div> </div> </div> </div>Did I say anyone had to explicitly incorporate Wraith before? They don't have to if they don't want to. But like <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar741_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"741\" data-vbnamecard=\"741\">AnubisXy</a> alluded to, it makes sense to look in Wraith for material to work with. It wouldn't hurt and a person who is interested in Necromancy to begin with, would probably be interested in Wraith for further reading anyway. It's very unlikely for someone, who goes to the specific Path to interact with ghosts over anything else, to not be interested it. Though of course, again, YMMV.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Necromancy's point definitely isn't to do this</div> </div> </div> </div>Can you explain what, specifically, what I said in anything so far that contradicts what Necromancy can do in any of my posts? <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Jade Kingdom Warrior","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25523-shakanaka","name":"Shakanaka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"951"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-03T07:23:21-07:00","id":1458509,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shakanaka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1458165-necromancy-or-thaumaturgy?p=1458493#post1458493\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Clearly the entire whole discussion was about Wraiths and the applications on how they should be used, if needed in a Vampire chronicle dealing with Necromancy. Even before you got involved in it, it was pretty explicitly touching base on Vampire/Wraith and its mechanics.\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>Nope. The whole discussion is about which among two disciplines you like more.<br /> <br /> Several posters said they like Necromancy because it opens up the possibility of using Wraith material, which is perfectly fine.<br /> <br /> But the heated discussion about Wraith that you engaged with began with post #13 by Black Fox, where they explicitly said that both options existed, both were valid, but there was a big problem with players trying to game it into being a superweapon. Their argument wasn't against using Wraiths and their points about Wraith were just illustrations on how to not let Necromancy be a superweapon all by itself.<br /> <br /> During this argument they cited your post, #4, where you praised the Discipline on the basis of how unstoppable an assassin a Wraith would be, giving an impression that exemplified their actual point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shakanaka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1458165-necromancy-or-thaumaturgy?p=1458493#post1458493\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But like <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar741_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"741\" data-vbnamecard=\"741\">AnubisXy</a> alluded to, it makes sense to look in Wraith for material to work with. It wouldn't hurt and a person who is interested in Necromancy to begin with, would probably be interested in Wraith for further reading anyway. It's very unlikely for someone, who goes to the specific Path to interact with ghosts over anything else, to not be interested it. Though of course, again, YMMV.\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>I know a lot of people that like Necromancy and, for one reason or another, didn't read Wraith. More importantly, a lot of players that like Necromancy play with STs that didn't read Wraith.<br /> <br /> So yeah, it makes sense to look at Wraith for material on ghosts, but it doesn't make sense to assume everyone does. And so it is a side discussion for this topic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shakanaka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1458165-necromancy-or-thaumaturgy?p=1458493#post1458493\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can you explain what, specifically, what I said in anything so far that contradicts what Necromancy can do in any of my posts?</div> </div> </div> </div>Let me be clear: I didn't meant that Necromancy can't do what you said. I meant that its goal isn't to bring Wraith to the table, no matter if this can be done or not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}